tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in the Research Counter. My research log has solidified my next step: to build a model that combines the strengths of my Deep Residual CNN with an attention mechanism to better handle long-range dependencies.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Adding a self-attention layer after the CNN feature extraction stage will allow the model to weigh the importance of different sequence regions dynamically. This will enable it to capture long-range interactions without the information bottleneck that caused the CNN-LSTM model to fail, leading to improved performance, especially on datasets with long sequences.\n\nI will now implement this architecture. It will use the same ResNet backbone as my best model (ID 6) and integrate a multi-head self-attention layer before the final pooling and prediction steps.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Attention-Augmented Residual CNN for RNA Modeling\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, attention, transformer, sequence\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission enhances\
    \ the high-performing Deep Residual CNN architecture with a multi-head self-attention mechanism. After the CNN backbone extracts a sequence of local feature representations, an attention layer is applied to model long-range dependencies by re-weighting these features based on their contextual importance. This approach is designed to overcome the limitations of a fixed receptive field in pure CNNs and the information bottleneck of simple RNNs, aiming for a more robust and generalizable model across all datasets, particularly those with long sequences.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_features': 128,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_res_blocks': 3,\n          'num_attention_heads':\
    \ 4\n      }\n\n  # --- Residual Block (from previous submission) ---\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          if x.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(x)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- Attention-Augmented CNN Model ---\n  class ResCnnAttentionRNA(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      num_res_blocks: int\n      num_attention_heads: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. CNN Feature Extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features,\
    \ kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          for _ in range(self.num_res_blocks):\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size)(x)\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, cnn_features)\n\n          # 2. Self-Attention Layer\n          # The input to attention is the sequence of features from the CNN\n          attn_output = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(\n              num_heads=self.num_attention_heads,\n              qkv_features=self.cnn_features\n          )(inputs_q=x, inputs_kv=x) # Self-attention\n          # attn_output shape: (batch, seq_len, cnn_features)\n          \n          # Add & Norm (common in transformer architectures)\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x + attn_output)\n\n          # 3. Global Max Pooling on the attention-processed features\n          x = jnp.max(x, axis=1)\n          # x shape: (batch, cnn_features)\n          \n          # 4. Dense layers for final prediction\n\
    \          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          \n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  # --- Network Wrapper ---\n  class ResCnnAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ResCnnAttentionRNA(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {}\n          if not deterministic:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key\
    \ = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              rngs['dropout'] = rng_key\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  # --- Factory Function ---\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResCnnAttentionWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n          task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n          cnn_features=hparams['cnn_features'],\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n          num_res_blocks=hparams['num_res_blocks'],\n          num_attention_heads=hparams['num_attention_heads'],\n          hidden_dim=hparams['hidden_dim'],\n          dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Promising New Architecture for ncRNA Classification**\n\nAlright, I'm back at the Research Counter. My next move, as I see it, is to build upon my successes with a novel architecture. The idea is to marry my strong Deep Residual CNN with a strategic attention mechanism. I'm aiming to create a model that learns long-range dependencies in ncRNA sequences more effectively than past attempts.\n\nMy reasoning is this: while the ResNet CNN (Model ID 6) excels at grabbing local features, it suffers from a limited receptive field. I tried a CNN-LSTM (Model ID 10) previously, but the compression into a single LSTM state created a significant bottleneck, especially with long sequences. \n\nThe attention mechanism, specifically self-attention, seems like the missing piece. It lets the model dynamically weigh the importance of different parts of the sequence, allowing it to look at the whole picture and identify key relationships. No more forced compression! The CNN will extract\
  \ features, and then the attention layer will figure out which features are most relevant for the prediction.\n\nHere's the plan: I'll start with the rock-solid code from my best-performing model, the Deep Residual CNN (ID 6). After the residual blocks do their thing, I'll insert a `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` layer using `flax.linen`. The output of this attention layer will be a re-weighted sequence of feature vectors, which I'll then process with global max pooling before hitting the final dense layers.\n\nThis setup should allow the model to effectively integrate information from the entire sequence. I'm feeling confident about this approach. It's time to get coding!\n"
