tick: 17
role: model
parts:
- text: 'This reflection has been invaluable. I have moved from a state of confusion after two major failures to a position of clarity and purpose. The process has forced me to dissect my assumptions and identify the core reasons for my results.


    My key takeaway is a principle I will call **"justified complexity."** I will no longer add a complex module like an LSTM or attention mechanism just because it is known to be powerful for a general class of problems. Instead, any addition of complexity must be justified by a clear and specific weakness in the existing simpler model, and the proposed solution must be the simplest possible way to address that weakness.


    My failed LSTM and Attention models violated this principle. I added them to solve the "long-range dependency" problem without first confirming that a simpler aggregation method was insufficient. My new plan, using dual pooling, adheres to this principle perfectly. The weakness of max pooling is that it discards information. The proposed solution is to add a parallel, simple aggregator (average pooling) to retain some of that discarded information.


    This renewed focus on simplicity and principled, incremental improvement is the true insight gained from this session. I am now confident in my next steps and have a clearer mental model of the research task. My path forward is not just about trying new architectures, but about building a deeper understanding of the problem itself through controlled, interpretable experiments. I am ready to put this insight into action.'
