tick: 21
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is a significant breakthrough. The Dilated ResNet CNN (ID 33) achieved a score of **0.6521**, a massive improvement over my previous best of 0.5994. My hypothesis that increasing the receptive field with dilated convolutions would capture long-range dependencies more effectively has been strongly validated.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe performance gains are dramatic and widespread, particularly on datasets that likely benefit from a wider contextual view: `APA`, `Modif`, `MRL`, and `ncRNA` all saw substantial score increases. This confirms that the model is now learning relationships between distant parts of the RNA sequence, something my previous models struggled with.\n\nHowever, the performance on the CRISPR-related datasets, `CRI-On` and `CRI-Off`, has either stagnated or slightly decreased. This is a recurring pattern. `CRI-Off` remains my most challenging dataset, showing almost no improvement since the very first CNN. This suggests that the features required to succeed\
    \ on these specific tasks may be fundamentally different from the others. They have the shortest sequence lengths, so perhaps the issue is not the receptive field, but the nature of the features themselves. The interaction between the sgRNA and the target sequence might require a different kind of architectural bias, perhaps one that explicitly models pairwise interactions.\n\n**Next Steps:**\nI have a very powerful general architecture now. Before I try to tackle the `CRI` dataset problem specifically, I should see if I can refine this successful architecture further. One area I have not touched is the optimizer. I have been using the system default (`adamw` with a fixed learning rate of 0.001 and weight decay of 0.01). It is possible that the training dynamics could be improved.\n\nA common technique for improving training stability and performance in deep networks is to use a learning rate scheduler. A scheduler that gradually warms up the learning rate and then decays it over time\
    \ can help the model settle into a better minimum.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Implementing a learning rate schedule with a warmup and cosine decay will improve the training process of the Dilated ResNet CNN, leading to better convergence and a higher final score. This is a refinement of the training procedure, not the architecture itself.\n\nI will submit the same Dilated ResNet CNN, but this time I will implement a custom `create_optimizer` function that includes a learning rate schedule.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated ResNet CNN with LR Schedule\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, dilated convolution, optimizer, learning rate schedule\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission builds on the highly successful Dilated ResNet CNN (ID 33) by refining the training procedure. Instead of a fixed learning rate, this experiment implements a custom optimizer with a learning rate schedule. The schedule includes a linear warmup phase followed by a cosine decay. This is hypothesized to improve model\
    \ training by allowing for more stable convergence, potentially leading to a better final performance without altering the core network architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, # This will be the peak learning rate\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_features': 128,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_res_blocks': 3,\n          'dilation_rates': [1, 2, 4],\n          'warmup_epochs': 5,\n          'total_epochs': 100 # Max epochs from task spec\n      }\n\n  # --- Custom Optimizer with LR Schedule ---\n  def create_optimizer(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      # Estimated steps per epoch, this is a rough guess.\n      # A more accurate value would require knowing dataset size, but this\
    \ is a reasonable heuristic.\n      # Based on logs, train sets are ~10k-150k, batch 64. So ~150-2400 steps/epoch. Let's use 1000.\n      steps_per_epoch = 1000\n      total_steps = hparams['total_epochs'] * steps_per_epoch\n      warmup_steps = hparams['warmup_epochs'] * steps_per_epoch\n\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=hparams['learning_rate'],\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps - warmup_steps,\n          end_value=0.0\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n\n  # --- Residual Block with Dilation (Identical to ID 33) ---\n  class DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilation: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), \n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), \n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          if x.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(x)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- Dilated ResNet CNN Model (Identical to ID 33) ---\n  class DilatedResNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      num_res_blocks: int\n      dilation_rates: Sequence[int]\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          for i in range(self.num_res_blocks):\n              dilation = self.dilation_rates[i] if i < len(self.dilation_rates) else self.dilation_rates[-1]\n\
    \              x = DilatedResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size, dilation=dilation)(x)\n          x = jnp.max(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  # --- Network Wrapper ---\n  class DilatedResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DilatedResNet(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True,\
    \ rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {}\n          if not deterministic:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              rngs['dropout'] = rng_key\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  # --- Factory Function ---\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return DilatedResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n          task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n          cnn_features=hparams['cnn_features'],\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n          num_res_blocks=hparams['num_res_blocks'],\n          dilation_rates=hparams['dilation_rates'],\n          hidden_dim=hparams['hidden_dim'],\n          dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n      )\n```"
