tick: 45
role: user
parts:
- text: '**Reflection Tick 2 / 5**'
---
tick: 45
role: model
parts:
- text: 'So, the first pitfall to avoid is ignoring sequence order. I must implement positional encodings. A simple approach is to use learned embeddings for each position, which are added to the one-hot nucleotide embeddings.


    The second major pitfall is the one that doomed my Hybrid 2D model: computational complexity. Standard self-attention has a memory and computational cost that scales quadratically with the sequence length (`O(L^2)`). The `ncRNA` dataset has sequences longer than 1000 bases. A full self-attention matrix for `L=1136` would be enormous (`1136x1136`), almost certainly causing another out-of-memory error.


    This means a naive, vanilla Transformer is not a viable option. I must use a more efficient variant of attention. There are many such variants, but a common and effective one is to use a "local" or "sliding window" attention. Instead of each position attending to every other position, it only attends to a window of positions around it (e.g., `k` positions to the left and `k` to the right). This restores a degree of locality bias, making it somewhat more like a CNN, but it''s a "soft" bias. The attention mechanism can still learn to ignore positions within the window, and by stacking these local attention layers, the effective receptive field grows, allowing the model to learn longer-range dependencies.


    This seems like a promising compromise: it''s more flexible than a CNN but more computationally tractable than a full Transformer. This will be my second key implementation detail: use a local or sliding-window self-attention mechanism.'
