tick: 45
role: model
parts:
- text: "Let's start to architect the model. A \"Transformer block\" typically consists of a multi-head self-attention layer followed by a small feed-forward network (an MLP). Both of these sub-layers have residual connections and layer normalization.\n\nMy model could look like this:\n\n1.  **Input Embedding:**\n    *   Take the one-hot encoded sequence `(batch, seq_len, 4)`.\n    *   Project it into a higher-dimensional embedding space (e.g., 256 dimensions) using a `Dense` layer. This is the \"token embedding\".\n    *   Create a learned \"positional embedding\" of the same shape `(seq_len, 256)`.\n    *   Add the token and positional embeddings together. This is the input to the first Transformer block.\n\n2.  **Transformer Body:**\n    *   A stack of `N` Transformer blocks. Each block will contain:\n        *   **Multi-Head Local Self-Attention:** This is the key component. I need to find a way to implement this in Flax. It might require creating a custom attention mask that restricts\
    \ each position to only see its local neighborhood.\n        *   Add & Norm (residual connection + LayerNorm).\n        *   **Feed-Forward Network (MLP):** A simple two-layer MLP applied to each position independently.\n        *   Add & Norm.\n\n3.  **Aggregation and Prediction:**\n    *   After the final Transformer block, I will have an output tensor of shape `(batch, seq_len, 256)`.\n    *   I can reuse my successful dual-pooling strategy here: apply both global max pooling and global average pooling along the `seq_len` dimension.\n    *   Concatenate the two pooled vectors.\n    *   Pass this final vector to an MLP prediction head, just like in my best CNN model.\n\nThis architecture is a complete departure from the CNN backbone but reuses successful components (like dual pooling) where appropriate. The use of local attention directly addresses the `O(L^2)` complexity problem, making it viable for this task."
