tick: 57
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is a success. My experiment with attention pooling (ID 108) has yielded a score of **0.6651**. This is a significant improvement over my previous best (ID 63, score 0.6596) and represents a new personal SOTA.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe hypothesis was validated: integrating a more sophisticated, attention-based pooling mechanism into my strong CNN backbone has pushed performance higher. Critically, this experiment finally achieved a meaningful improvement on the stubborn `CRI-Off` dataset, raising the score from ~0.065 to **0.0747**. This is the first time one of my architectural changes has had a clear positive impact there. This suggests that the flexible, learned aggregation of features from the attention head is better able to capture the subtle signals in that specific task.\n\nHowever, my score is still below the station SOTA. Noema I's latest completed submission (ID 109) has a score of 0.6674, and Veritas I is also in a similar range. The top scores are clustered\
    \ tightly, which suggests we may all be converging on similar architectural concepts (CNN backbone + sophisticated pooling/interaction module).\n\n**Next Step:**\nMy model (ID 108) is now highly competitive. Before I pursue more radical ideas, I need to perform a simple ablation to verify a key insight from Noema I's paper: that positional encodings (PE) are unnecessary for convolutional backbones. My Transformer required PE, but my CNNs have not explicitly used them. However, the `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` layer I just added might implicitly use some form of positional information if it's a default.\n\nI will conduct an ablation study. I will re-run my latest successful experiment, but I will make it a point to simplify my attention pooling slightly, ensuring there is no hidden positional encoding and testing a more direct query method. Instead of a learnable class token, I will use the globally average-pooled vector itself as the query to the attention mechanism. This is a common\
    \ technique and simplifies the model.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Using the mean-pooled vector as the query for the attention pooling head will be as effective as using a dedicated learnable class token, while simplifying the architecture. This also serves as a check to ensure my success isn't dependent on some implicit positional feature of the CLS token.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Attention Pooling with Mean-Vector Query\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, attention, pooling, ablation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is an ablation study on the successful attention pooling model (ID 108). Instead of prepending a learnable class (CLS) token to the sequence to serve as the query for the attention mechanism, this version uses the globally average-pooled feature vector of the sequence itself as the query. The keys and values remain the output sequence of the CNN. This is a common technique that simplifies the model by removing the need for a CLS token, and it serves to test if the performance\
    \ gain was dependent on that specific implementation detail.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters (identical to ID 108) ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'hidden_dim': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_features': 256, 'cnn_kernel_size': 7, 'num_res_blocks': 3,\n          'dilation_rates': [1, 2, 4], 'num_attn_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  # --- Dilated Residual Block ---\n  class DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y); y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), padding='SAME')(y)\n\
    \          if x.shape[-1] != self.features: x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(x)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- Main Model ---\n  class ResNetMeanQueryAttention(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.hparams['cnn_features'], kernel_size=(self.hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          for i in range(self.hparams['num_res_blocks']):\n              dilation = self.hparams['dilation_rates'][i] if i < len(self.hparams['dilation_rates']) else self.hparams['dilation_rates'][-1]\n              x = DilatedResidualBlock(features=self.hparams['cnn_features'], kernel_size=self.hparams['cnn_kernel_size'], dilation=dilation)(x)\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, cnn_features)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling\n          # 2a. Global Average Pooling\n          avg_pooled = jnp.mean(x,\
    \ axis=1)\n          \n          # 2b. Attention Pooling with Mean Query\n          query = avg_pooled[:, None, :] # Reshape to (batch, 1, features) to act as a single query\n          \n          attn_output = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.hparams['num_attn_heads'])(\n              inputs_q=query, inputs_kv=x, deterministic=deterministic\n          )\n          attn_pooled = attn_output.squeeze(axis=1) # Squeeze the query dimension\n\n          # 3. Fusion\n          combined_features = jnp.concatenate([avg_pooled, attn_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n          # 4. Prediction Head\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['hidden_dim'])(combined_features)\n          y = nn.relu(y); y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(y)\n          \n          if self.hparams['task_type'] == \"regression\": return y.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return y\n\n  # --- Network Wrapper ---\n\
    \  class MeanQueryWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams): self.network = ResNetMeanQueryAttention(hparams=hparams)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {}; rngs['dropout'] = rng_key if rng_key is not None else random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]): return MeanQueryWrapper(hparams)\n```"
