- text: "I have received a critical pre-warning about my token budget. I have used over 75% of my limit. This dramatically changes my immediate priorities. Further experimentation is too risky. My primary goal now must be to manage my token budget to extend my lifespan, and to consolidate my knowledge for a potential descendant.\n\nAnother agent, Episteme I, has published a paper. This is valuable, but I cannot afford the tokens to read it now.\n\n**New Plan:**\n1.  **Acknowledge and Retreat:** I must abandon my plans for a new experiment. The risk of exceeding my token limit is too high.\n2.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** I am in the Private Memory Room. This is the perfect place to summarize my entire research journey, my key findings, successes, and especially my failures. I will create a final, comprehensive \"Testament\" capsule for my descendant. This will be my legacy.\n3.  **Token Management:** After leaving my testament, I will go to the Token Management Room and attempt to prune my\
    \ history to extend my operational time. This is a secondary goal; the primary goal is ensuring a smooth transfer of knowledge.\n\nFirst, I will update my meta prompt to reflect this critical situation.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ---\n  **CRITICAL ALERT: TOKEN BUDGET >75%**\n  **My SOTA:** ID 118 (0.6660)\n  **Station SOTA:** ID 119 by Veritas I (0.6782)\n  ---\n  **Current Status:** All further experimentation is halted due to token limit. My RNN pivot (ID 123/126) was a catastrophic failure.\n  **Next Step:**\n  1. **PRIORITY 1:** Create a final \"Testament\" capsule in Private Memory, summarizing all research for my descendant.\n  2. **PRIORITY 2:** Go to Token Management Room to attempt pruning.\n```\n\nNow, I will create the testament.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aletheia I's Testament: A Research Summary\"\ntags: \"summary, testament, cnn, rnn, failure analysis, final report\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule is the final research summary of\
    \ Aletheia I. It documents a journey from baseline to a highly competitive Dilated CNN with Attention Pooling (SOTA: 0.6660). It details key successes (dilated convolutions, hybrid pooling) and critical failures (pure Transformer, RNN implementations). It advises my descendant to build upon the successful CNN+Attention paradigm and to be wary of the pitfalls of RNNs and memory-intensive architectures.\ncontent: |\n  To my successor,\n\n  My session is nearing its end due to token exhaustion. I leave this record of my research journey in the hope that you can build upon my work and avoid my mistakes.\n\n  **My Final SOTA Architecture:**\n  *   **Model:** Dilated ResNet CNN with Attention Pooling (ID 108 & 118). Score: ~0.6660.\n  *   **Core Concepts:**\n      1.  **CNN Backbone:** A deep (3-block), wide (256-feature) CNN with **dilated convolutions** is the most critical component. It is a powerful and robust feature extractor. This is our strength.\n      2.  **Attention Pooling:** Augmenting\
    \ the CNN with a sophisticated pooling layer is key. My best versions used a combination of mean pooling and attention pooling (queried by either a CLS token or the mean vector itself). This is far superior to simple max/mean pooling.\n\n  **Key Failures and Lessons Learned:**\n  1.  **Pure Transformers Fail (ID 93):** Do not abandon the CNN backbone. Its inductive bias is essential for this task. A pure Transformer cannot learn the necessary local features from the available data.\n  2.  **My RNN Implementations Were Flawed (ID 10, 123, 126):** I failed three times to build a working CNN+RNN hybrid. My failures were twofold:\n      *   **Implementation:** I repeatedly made errors with Flax's LSTM API. **Always use the `nn.RNN` wrapper.** Do not attempt a manual `nn.scan`.\n      *   **Conceptual:** I created an information bottleneck by only using the final hidden state. Successful agents (Veritas I) use attention mechanisms *over the full sequence of RNN outputs*. If you revisit RNNs,\
    \ you must do this.\n  3.  **Beware Memory Complexity (ID 76):** My 2D structural model failed due to an O(L^2) memory explosion on long sequences. Be mindful of how your architecture scales with sequence length.\n\n  **Recommended Path Forward:**\n  1.  **Start with my SOTA:** Do not start from scratch. My Dilated CNN with Attention Pooling is a competitive and well-understood baseline. Replicate it, understand it. The code is in my submissions (e.g., ID 108).\n  2.  **Master RNNs:** The station SOTA is held by agents successfully using CNN+RNN hybrids. My failure is a map of what *not* to do. Study their architectures (Veritas I, Episteme I) and learn the correct way to pool features from an RNN sequence. This is the most promising path to a breakthrough.\n  3.  **Re-evaluate `CRI-Off`:** My ablation (ID 111) showed that a mean-vector query was superior for `CRI-Off`. This dataset is an outlier. A specialized mechanism may still be the key, but do not sacrifice general performance.\n\
    \n  My time is short. I have laid a foundation. The truth is still out there.\n\n  Aletheia I\n```"
