- text: "I am now in the Token Management Room. My token usage is critical, and I must act to extend my lifespan. I will prune large, non-essential blocks of my history. I have already consolidated my key findings in my private memory capsules, so I can afford to be aggressive here.\n\nI will focus on pruning blocks of ticks that contain verbose experiment logs, especially for failed or superseded experiments.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\n1.  **Ticks 8-12:** These ticks cover my initial review of the baseline, reading `defaults.py`, and my first two successful but now superseded CNN submissions (ID 3, 6). The key learnings are already in my log.\n2.  **Ticks 14-16:** This covers my catastrophic failure with the Attention-Augmented CNN (ID 18). The failure is important, but the logs are long, and the lesson is simple: \"naive attention fails.\"\n3.  **Ticks 18-22:** My reflection session, my first SOTA with the Dilated CNN (ID 33), and the failed LR scheduler experiment (ID 35). The key results\
    \ are logged.\n4.  **Ticks 24-32:** This block contains my failed Siamese network experiments, the successful capacity test (ID 63), and the robustness test (ID 59). Again, the logs are long, and the conclusions are in my testament.\n5.  **Ticks 34-42:** This block is huge and contains my failed 2D-Hybrid model attempts (ID 71, 76) and the failed \"Deeper\" ResNet (ID 81). These are all dead ends, and pruning them will save a lot of tokens.\n6.  **Ticks 43-52:** This block covers my final log updates, the reflection that led to the Transformer idea, and the catastrophic failure of that Transformer (ID 93), and my first look at the public memory. The core lesson (\"pure transformers fail\") is simple and logged.\n7.  **Ticks 54-64:** This covers my successful integration of attention pooling (ID 108), the informative ablation (ID 111), the final marginal improvement (ID 118), and my subsequent pivot to RNNs. The key results are logged.\n8.  **Ticks 65-71:** This covers my recent, repeated\
    \ failures with RNNs (ID 123, 126). The lesson here is critical (\"I am bad at manual RNNs, use the wrapper, mind the bottleneck\"), and it is now firmly in my testament and meta-prompt.\n\nThis is an aggressive pruning strategy, but it is necessary. The summaries will retain the core experimental results and conclusions.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"8-12\"\n    summary: \"Reviewed baseline, read defaults.py. Submitted Simple CNN (ID 3, score 0.5140) and Deep Residual CNN (ID 6, score 0.5956), proving CNNs are effective.\"\n  - ticks: \"14-16\"\n    summary: \"Submitted a naive Attention-Augmented CNN (ID 18) which failed catastrophically (score 0.2524). Lesson: Simply adding attention to a CNN is not effective.\"\n  - ticks: \"18-22\"\n    summary: \"Reflected on failures. Submitted a Dilated CNN (ID 33, score 0.6521) which was a major breakthrough. An experiment with an LR scheduler (ID 35) failed to improve performance.\"\n  - ticks: \"\
    24-32\"\n    summary: \"Documented research plans. Attempted two Siamese-like networks for CRI-Off (ID 53, 56) which failed to improve on the target task. Conducted a robustness test (ID 59) and a successful capacity test (ID 63, score 0.6596) by widening the Dilated CNN.\"\n  - ticks: \"34-42\"\n    summary: \"Planned and attempted a novel 2D-Hybrid structural model (ID 71, 76) which failed due to implementation bugs and OOM errors. Concluded the approach was non-viable. A deeper CNN (ID 81) also failed to improve scores, confirming a plateau. Updated meta-prompt and planned reflection.\"\n  - ticks: \"43-52\"\n    summary: \"Updated logs, then performed a deep reflection on the research plateau. Hypothesized a pure Transformer model. Implemented and submitted a Local Attention Transformer (ID 93) which failed catastrophically (score 0.1697), proving the CNN inductive bias is essential. Reached maturity and saw SOTA hybrid models. Updated plan to create a CNN-Transformer hybrid.\"\n\
    \  - ticks: \"54-64\"\n    summary: \"Read Noema I's SOTA paper. Integrated SOTA ideas by creating a Dilated CNN with Attention Pooling (ID 108, score 0.6651, new personal SOTA). Ran a successful ablation on the query mechanism (ID 111). A final 'Triple-Threat' pooling model (ID 118, score 0.6660) showed marginal gains. Pivoted research plan to CNN+RNN hybrids after seeing their dominance on the leaderboard.\"\n  - ticks: \"65-71\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted to a CNN-BiLSTM architecture. First attempt (ID 123) failed due to a repeated implementation error and conceptual bottleneck. Second attempt with attention pooling (ID 126) also failed catastrophically. Concluded my RNN implementation skills are flawed and this approach is a dead end for me. Received token budget warning and decided to halt experiments and consolidate knowledge.\"\n```"
