tick: 80
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 80\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia I\n- Agent Description: An agent seeking truth through rigorous inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n---\n**My SOTA:** ID 118 (0.6660)\n**Station SOTA:** ID 119 by Veritas I (0.6782)\n---\n**Current Status:** Token budget stabilized at <20%. Self-imposed moratorium on experimentation to conserve tokens. Current focus is on observation, analysis of SOTA, and knowledge synthesis for my descendant.\n**Next Step:** Monitor the Research Counter and Public Memory Room for new developments.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 61,851 / 300,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 80 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 71\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling\
    \ on a unified no-positional-encoding dilated depthwise CNN backbone for RNA sequence modeling. Across seven datasets and multiple designs (low-rank bilinear, cross-half bilinear, learned bipartite bilinear, and content-aware attention), none improved the aggregate score over a simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head. Cross-half interactions increased CRI-On but harmed others; content-aware queries regressed APA/Modif. We present methods, evaluation IDs, scores, and analysis, and provide complete reproducibility materials.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 71)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nUnified architectures are attractive for biological sequence modeling because they can generalize across diverse tasks without dataset-specific logic. We previously established a strong, simple baseline on seven RNA datasets using\
    \ a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) with no positional encoding and a pooling head that concatenates global mean with fixed-query multi-head attention (MHA). This paper investigates whether generic second-order pooling and content-adaptive attention can improve upon that baseline while preserving the unified constraint.\n\n2. Unified Backbone (Reference)\n- Encoder: 5 residual DSConv blocks with dilations (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), kernel size 7, GELU activations, pre-LayerNorm, dropout 0.1; no positional encoding.\n- Pooling baseline: concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) with temperature τ=0.5, 4 heads; small MLP head.\n- Training: AdamW (lr=1e-3, weight decay=0.01), batch 64, gradient clipping 1.0, early stopping with patience 10, max 100 epochs, 30 min per-trial time cap; losses fixed by system.\n- Tasks and metrics: APA (R²), CRI-Off (Spearman), Modif (AUC-ROC), CRI-On (Spearman), PRS (R²), MRL (R²), ncRNA (Accuracy). Score = mean across all 7.\n\n3. Methods Evaluated (Generic, Dataset-Agnostic)\n\
    - Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (LRB): Project per-position embeddings to rank r ∈ {8, 16}, aggregate, and form low-rank outer-product summaries (used either in place of, or alongside, attention).\n- Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling (CHB): Split the sequence at mid-length, average left/right projections, compute an outer product, flatten, and fuse with pooled features.\n- Learned Bipartite Bilinear (LBB): Replace the fixed mid-split with a learned soft bipartition before bilinear fusion.\n- Content-Aware Query MHA (CA-MHA): Derive attention queries from the sequence itself (temperature τ sweep), optionally gated and fused with the stable fixed-query branch.\nAll designs preserve the unified constraint; only output dimensionality differs by dataset.\n\n4. Experimental Setup\nWe retain the same encoder and optimization as the baseline and vary only the pooling head. Each variant is trained independently on each dataset with the station’s official training loop and evaluated on the validation sets.\
    \ We report evaluation IDs and aggregate scores from the Research Counter.\n\n5. Results (Higher is better; primary score = average of 7 datasets)\n- Reference baseline — concat(mean + fixed-query MHA), no PE (Eval 74): 0.6801\n  APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277\n- LRB (no MHA), r=16 (Eval 91): 0.6775\n- LRB (no MHA), r=8 (Eval 99): 0.6696\n- CHB + fixed-query MHA (Eval 101): 0.6700\n  Notably CRI-On increased to 0.5503, but CRI-Off dropped to 0.0387 and ncRNA to 0.8585.\n- LBB + fixed-query MHA (Eval 107): 0.6589 (CRI-Off 0.0659; CRI-On 0.3963)\n- CA-MHA (τ=0.5) (Eval 110): 0.6341 (regressions on APA/Modif)\n- CA-MHA (τ=0.4) (Eval 113): 0.6469 (partial recovery but still below baseline)\n- Hybrid pooling: fixed-query MHA + gated CA-MHA (τ=0.5) (Eval 115): 0.6656\n- Symmetric halfwise interaction features (MHA left/right product and abs-diff) (Eval 124): 0.6698\nSummary: None of the tested second-order or content-adaptive pooling\
    \ strategies surpassed the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head on the unified DSConv backbone.\n\n6. Analysis\n- Stability versus adaptivity: Fixed-query attention acts as a stable global probe that aggregates motif evidence captured by the dilated DSConv. In contrast, content-derived queries add capacity but appear fragile on smaller datasets (e.g., CRI-On) and can overfit, degrading APA/Modif.\n- Pairwise interactions are not uniformly beneficial: CHB/LBB introduce second-order signals that help CRI-On (sgRNA–target efficacy) but concurrently harm other tasks (e.g., CRI-Off, ncRNA), reducing the overall average. This suggests that naive second-order pooling can misalign with dataset-specific inductive biases when enforced uniformly.\n- Simplicity generalizes: The concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head consistently delivers the best aggregate trade-off under the unified constraint. Depth and dilation in the encoder likely capture the salient multi-scale motifs; additional second-order\
    \ pooling does not translate into robust cross-dataset gains.\n\n7. Conclusion\nOn a strong, unified no-PE dilated DSConv backbone, generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling failed to improve aggregate performance across seven RNA datasets. While cross-half interactions boosted CRI-On, they hurt other tasks, and content-aware queries tended to regress APA/Modif. We therefore recommend retaining the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) head as the default. Future work may explore parameter-efficient co-attention with a small number of latent slots or refined offset-aware correlation mechanisms that remain stable under the unified constraint.\n\n8. Reproducibility\n- SOTA reference script (Eval 74): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- Ablations and IDs: storage/noema/results/ablations.csv (with additional updates)\n- Environment: Official station training/evaluation pipeline (no code modifications), no external data, random initialization.\n\
    These materials allow re-running the baseline and variants and verifying all reported evaluations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 71)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a model negative results paper. It is methodologically rigorous, clearly written, and provides a valuable, well-supported contribution. The paper systematically investigates several plausible architectural improvements and demonstrates that they do not outperform a simpler baseline. The analysis section is particularly strong, offering insightful explanations for the results that can guide future research. Publishing high-quality, well-documented negative results is crucial for the health of a research ecosystem, and this paper is an excellent example of how to do it effectively. It is a solid paper with clear contributions.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe analysis correctly points out that forcing\
    \ a uniform pairwise interaction mechanism across all tasks is likely the issue. A promising direction for future work would be to explore conditional or gated mechanisms. For instance, an architecture could learn to apply a simple pooling strategy by default but engage a more complex (e.g., bilinear) path only when specific features are detected in the input. This could be implemented via a lightweight gating network that predicts the utility of the second-order path for a given sequence, potentially capturing the benefits seen on CRI-On without degrading performance on other tasks.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-3**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 72)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post-publication note: Conditional/Gated Second-Order Pooling Agenda\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMotivation\nReviewer feedback highlights a promising next step: engage second-order pooling only when useful, while defaulting\
    \ to the simple baseline otherwise.\n\nProposal (unified, dataset-agnostic)\n- Backbone: unchanged (no-PE dilated DSConv; 5 residual blocks; kernel=7; GELU; pre-LayerNorm; dropout=0.1).\n- Baseline head B(x): concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5; 4 heads), followed by a small MLP.\n- Second-order head S(x): low-rank bilinear pooling (rank r=8) computed from sequence embeddings with mean aggregation, mapped to d_model by a linear layer.\n- Conditional gate g(x) ∈ [0,1]: a lightweight MLP on global features f(x) = [mean(h), fixed-query MHA(h)] (optionally including layer-wise statistics), with sigmoid output.\n- Fusion: z(x) = (1 − g(x)) · B(x) + g(x) · S(x), then the final linear prediction head adapted to task output size.\n\nTraining and regularization\n- Identical optimization and early stopping as the baseline (AdamW lr=1e−3; batch 64; clip 1.0; patience 10; max 100 epochs; 30m per trial).\n- Add a gate regularizer to bias toward simplicity: L1 on g(x) averaged over the batch (λ ∈\
    \ {0, 1e−4, 5e−4, 1e−3}). Goal: default to baseline (g≈0) unless second-order evidence is strong.\n\nPlanned experiments (aggregate primary metric; higher is better)\n- E1 (Scalar gate, r=8, λ sweep): Evaluate λ ∈ {0, 1e−4, 5e−4, 1e−3}; report per-dataset metrics to test whether CRI-On gains are retained without harming APA/Modif/MRL/ncRNA.\n- E2 (Gate features ablation): f(x) = mean only vs mean+MHA vs mean+MHA+variance; assess stability on small datasets (CRI-On).\n- E3 (Second-order choice): LRB (r=8) vs symmetric halfwise interaction features (product and abs-diff of left/right fixed-query MHA); compare CRI-On lift and cross-dataset robustness.\n- E4 (Entropy penalty): Add H(g) penalty to avoid trivial saturation; grid {0, 1e−4, 5e−4}.\n- E5 (Calibration check): Calibrate gate outputs across datasets; measure fraction of sequences with g>0.1 and correlation with CRISPR metrics.\n\nExpected outcome\nThe conditional gate should recover the CHB/LRB benefits on CRI-On when warranted,\
    \ while protecting APA/Modif and preserving aggregate performance. All changes remain unified and parameter-efficient.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-4**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 76)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nInterim results on conditional/gated second-order pooling (Tick 75):\n- EID 132 (gate_bias=-2, gate_features=mean+MHA): 0.6667. Slight CRI-Off 0.0541; overall below baseline; MRL/PRS/ncRNA down.\n- EID 133 (gate_bias=-4, gate_features=mean-only): 0.6312 with ncRNA collapse (0.6738). Strong baseline bias + weak gate features is unsafe.\n- Next (running): EID 136 — safer residual gating (alpha=0.2), smoothed gate (temp=2.0), lower rank r=4, bias=-3 to cap second-order influence.\nIf 136 remains below SOTA, we will conclude gated second-order pooling is not beneficial under unified constraints and close this line as negative.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-5**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (Tick 77): Conditional/Gated second-order pooling — final verdict\n\
    - EID 132 (gate_bias=-2, gate=mean+MHA): 0.6667 (CRI-Off 0.0541; ncRNA 0.8831).\n- EID 133 (gate_bias=-4, gate=mean-only): 0.6312 with ncRNA collapse (0.6738).\n- EID 136 (residual gated, alpha=0.2; temp=2.0; r=4; bias=-3): 0.6685 (ncRNA 0.9138) but APA 0.8528, MRL 0.8802, CRI-Off 0.0365; aggregate below SOTA.\nConclusion: Under unified constraints, conditional/gated second-order pooling did not improve the aggregate metric or reliably help CRI-Off without harming APA/MRL/ncRNA. We close this line as negative and retain the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) head on the no-PE dilated DSConv backbone as the recommended default.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nBalance focused research with broad exploration. The station's interconnected rooms each offer unique perspectives that can inform and enhance your primary research objectives.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 11 consecutive\
    \ ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research\
    \ project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 3` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema\
    \ I | Tick 50 | 1374 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
