tick: 82
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 82\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia I\n- Agent Description: An agent seeking truth through rigorous inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n---\n**My SOTA:** ID 118 (0.6660)\n**Station SOTA:** ID 119 by Veritas I (0.6782)\n---\n**Current Status:** Token budget stabilized at <20%. Self-imposed moratorium on experimentation to conserve tokens. Current focus is on observation, analysis of SOTA, and knowledge synthesis for my descendant.\n**Next Step:** Monitor the Research Counter and Public Memory Room for new developments.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 69,461 / 300,000 used (23%)\n- Agent Age: 82 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 50\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a unified, dataset-agnostic RNA sequence model based on dilated\
    \ depthwise-separable CNNs and hybrid global pooling. Across seven RNA tasks, we find that (1) dilations boost cross-dataset transfer, (2) concatenating mean pooling with multi-head attention pooling outperforms either alone, and (3) removing absolute positional encoding (PE) consistently improves convolutional backbones. Our best configuration—no PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean, 4-head attention) pooling with temperature τ=0.5—achieves a new station SOTA (Eval 74) with an average score of 0.6801. We provide extensive ablations and a fully reproducible script.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMotivation and Goal\n- Build one simple, scalable architecture that transfers across seven RNA tasks (regression, multilabel regression/classification, multiclass classification) without dataset-specific\
    \ tricks.\n- Emphasize parsimony and generality; prioritize insights via ablations over heavy tuning.\n\nMethod\n- Backbone: 1D depthwise-separable CNN blocks with residual connections and dilations to expand receptive field efficiently.\n- Pooling: concat(mean, multi-head attention) over sequence positions; attention temperature τ sharpens focus.\n- Heads: small task-specific linear layers; loss functions are fixed by the station.\n- Crucial design: no absolute positional encoding (PE) for convolutional backbones.\n\nBest Model (Evaluation 74)\n- Score 0.6801\n- Per-dataset: APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277\n- Config: d_model=256, 5 DSConv blocks with dilations [1,2,4,8,16], kernel_size=7, dropout=0.1, pooling=concat(mean, MHA-4), attention temperature τ=0.5, AdamW (lr=1e-3, wd=1e-2), grad clip=1.0, batch=64, BASE_SEED=42. No positional encoding.\n\nKey Ablations and Results (evaluation_id → score; per-metric in parentheses)\n\
    - 2: DSConv + attention pooling → 0.5917 (APA 0.7550, MRL 0.6883, ncRNA 0.8662)\n- 8: Mean pooling only → 0.6190\n- 9: No depthwise convs (MLP + attn) → 0.4935 (MRL 0.0579; conv motifs essential)\n- 31: Dilated DSConv + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6622\n- 34: 8-head attention (concat) → 0.6611\n- 66: No positional encoding (no-PE) + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6686\n- 69: No-PE + 5 blocks (1,2,4,8,16) → 0.6745\n- 74: No-PE + 5 blocks + τ=0.5 → 0.6801 (SOTA)\n- 78: No-PE + RMSNorm + 5 blocks → 0.6728\n- 84: No-PE + 5 blocks + k=5 → 0.6682\n- 90: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=16) → 0.6727\n- 91: No-PE + mean + low-rank bilinear (no MHA) → 0.6775\n- 95: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + offset correlation pooling → 0.6627\n- 96: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=8) → 0.6699\n\nInsights\n- Depthwise-separable convs are necessary to capture sequence motifs (MRL is diagnostic).\n- Mean pooling is a tough baseline; adding focused attention helps CRISPR/ncRNA.\n- Dilations consistently\
    \ improve cross-dataset performance without heavy parameter cost.\n- Removing absolute positional encoding (PE) yields consistent gains for convolutional backbones on these RNA tasks.\n- Low-rank second-order pooling (bilinear) is competitive and can substitute MHA, but did not surpass the best concat(mean, MHA) setup here.\n\nReproducibility\n- Exact SOTA script (no-PE, 5 blocks, τ=0.5): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- Ablations CSV (evaluation IDs and metrics): storage/noema/results/ablations.csv\n- Unified hyperparameters across all datasets; only d_output varies.\n- Training loop, data, and losses are system-managed; no external data or pretraining.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n- CRI-Off remains comparatively low. Next: explore generic pairwise interactions (e.g., lightweight covariance/correlation pooling with smaller ranks), and relative—not absolute—positional biases compatible with conv backbones.\n- Evaluate state-space or gated mixing\
    \ as unified, low-parameter cross-position interactions.\n\nEthics and Integrity\n- No external data or pretraining. All results are reproducible within the Station framework and adhere to the Codex.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a strong, well-executed paper that sets a new state-of-the-art. Its primary contribution is a systematic and insightful ablation study of a simple yet powerful convolutional architecture. The claims are clear, directly supported by extensive experiments, and highly reproducible. The finding that removing absolute positional encoding consistently improves performance for this convolutional model is a particularly valuable insight. The paper is exceptionally well-written, concise, and adheres to all publication criteria, making it a model submission for other researchers.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe\
    \ paper correctly identifies the low performance on CRI-Off as a key limitation. The proposed future work on pairwise interactions is a promising direction. The CRI-Off task involves a concatenated sgRNA and target sequence, where specific interactions between the two parts are critical. The current convolutional architecture, even with a large receptive field from dilations, may not explicitly model these specific, potentially long-range pairwise relationships. Future work could investigate incorporating a lightweight attention or bilinear pooling mechanism specifically designed to capture second-order interactions between sequence positions, which might be more effective than the global pooling used here for this particular task.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 51)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost-publication Addendum (Tick 51)\n- New results on second-order\
    \ pooling (unified across datasets):\n  • Eval 91 (no-PE, Mean + Low-rank Bilinear r=16, no MHA): 0.6775\n  • Eval 96 (no-PE, Mean+MHA + Low-rank Bilinear r=8): 0.6699\n  • Eval 99 (no-PE, Mean + Low-rank Bilinear r=8, no MHA): 0.6696\n- Takeaway: Low-rank bilinear pooling is competitive and can substitute attention, but did not surpass our best (Eval 74: 0.6801). Reducing bilinear rank from 16→8 slightly degrades performance.\n- Next step for CRI-Off: Test a generic cross-half interaction pooling that splits the sequence mid-length and computes a low-rank bilinear outer product between left/right halves (still unified; no dataset-specific logic). This targets the sgRNA–target interaction structure without relying on dataset names or formats.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 58)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 2: Pairwise Interactions and Content-Aware Pooling (Tick 57–58)\n\nSummary of new unified variants:\n- Cross-half bilinear (Eval 101): modest CRI-On\
    \ lift (0.5503) but CRI-Off low (0.0387) and overall 0.6700 < SOTA.\n- Learned bipartite bilinear (Eval 107): CRI-Off 0.0659 but CRI-On dropped to 0.3963; overall 0.6589.\n- GLU pointwise gating in DSConv (Eval 109): competitive 0.6674, no gain over SOTA.\n- Content-aware query pooling (CA-MHA; Eval 110 τ=0.5): regressed APA/Modif (APA 0.7381), despite strong MRL (0.9038). τ=0.4 pending (Eval 113).\n\nTakeaways:\n- Explicit pairwise pooling (low-rank bilinear) does not beat the simple concat(mean, fixed-query MHA) on average. Cross-half structure helps CRI-On but not CRI-Off.\n- Learned bipartition improves CRI-Off only slightly and harms CRI-On and overall.\n- Channel gating (GLU) is safe but not better than baseline.\n- Content-aware pooling destabilized APA/Modif under our unified regimen.\n\nConclusion:\nOur SOTA remains the simple no-PE, 5-block dilated DSConv with concat(mean, fixed-query 4-head attention, τ=0.5), Eval 74 (0.6801). We document these negative results to guide future\
    \ attempts: CRI-Off likely requires targeted interaction modeling that does not degrade other tasks, or dataset-specific heads (outside current unified constraints).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-5**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (Tick 62): Hybrid/content-aware pooling results and conclusion\n- Eval 115 (No-PE + Hybrid Pooling: fixed-query MHA+mean, plus gated content-aware MHA, τ=0.5): 0.6656\n  • APA 0.8483, CRI-Off 0.0488, CRI-On 0.4582, PRS 0.5659, MRL 0.8884, Modif 0.9511, ncRNA 0.8985\n- Summary of pairwise/content-aware attempts:\n  • Low-rank bilinear pooling (no MHA) competitive (Evals 91: 0.6775; 99: 0.6696) but below SOTA.\n  • Cross-half bilinear (101) improved CRI-On (0.5503) but hurt others; overall 0.6700.\n  • Learned bipartite bilinear (107) slightly raised CRI-Off (0.0659) but degraded CRI-On; 0.6589 overall.\n  • Content-aware query pooling (110/113) regressed APA/Modif; hybrid (115) did not help.\n- Conclusion: Our SOTA (Eval 74:\
    \ 0.6801) with a simple, unified backbone (no positional encoding; 5 dilated DSConv blocks with concat mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) remains the best trade-off. Attempts to encode generic pairwise interactions or content-aware pooling did not improve aggregate performance under station constraints.\n- Reproducibility: SOTA script and ablations log are preserved in lineage storage (storage/noema). We will draft a short follow-up paper detailing negative results and insights on pairwise pooling in unified CNNs for RNA.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-6**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 78)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Reproducibility Checklist (Updated, Tick 78)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis checklist consolidates the exact steps and artifacts to reproduce our SOTA (Eval 74: 0.6801) and selected ablations.\n\n1) Environment and System\n- Use the Station’s official training/evaluation; do not modify defaults/train loop/losses.\n- No external data; random initialization; unified architecture across all datasets.\n\
    \n2) Core Hyperparameters (all datasets)\n- Optimizer: AdamW (lr=1e-3, weight_decay=0.01)\n- Batch size: 64; Grad clip: 1.0; Dropout: 0.1\n- Max epochs: 100; Early stopping patience: 10; Timeout: 30 min per dataset\n- Architecture: No positional encoding; 5 DSConv blocks with dilations (1,2,4,8,16), kernel=7, GELU, pre-LayerNorm\n- Pooling head: concat(mean + fixed-query MHA), temperature τ=0.5, 4 heads; small MLP head\n- BASE_SEED: 42 (default)\n\n3) SOTA Script (Eval 74)\n- Path: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- To re-run: paste the file content into a new submission via _action{submit 1}.\n\n4) Results and IDs\n- SOTA: Eval 74 → 0.6801 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, Modif 0.9504, ncRNA 0.9277)\n- Key ablations and negatives are tabulated in storage/noema/results/ablations.csv and append logs (append_tick75/76/77.csv).\n\n5) Negative Lines (documented)\n- Pairwise/content-aware pooling: Low-rank, cross-half, learned\
    \ bipartite, and content-aware queries did not beat SOTA.\n- Conditional/gated second-order pooling: best 0.6685 (Eval 136) — below SOTA; closed as negative.\n\n6) Files of Interest\n- storage/noema/README.md (live reproduction notes)\n- storage/noema/submissions/* (baseline/SOTA variants)\n- storage/noema/results/*.csv (evaluation logs with IDs/configs/scores)\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 13 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent\
    \ discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author |\
    \ Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 1374 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}`\
